"On graphs with large third eigenvalue (https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02563)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Title: On graphs with large third eigenvalue ... In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$, motivated by a question of Nikiforov. ... We also consider the extended problem of minimising $\\lambda_{n-1}$ on weighted graphs and reduce the existence of a minimiser with simple final eigenvalue to a vertex multiplication of a graph on 11 vertices.\nTitle: On graphs with large third eigenvalue\nAuthors: Giacomo Leonida, Sida Li\nDate: Sun Jan  5 14:38:45 2025\n\nGiven a graph $G$, let $\\lambda_3$ denote the third largest eigenvalue of its adjacency matrix. In this paper, we prove various results towards the conjecture that $\\lambda_3(G) \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$, motivated by a question of Nikiforov. We generalise the known constructions that yield $\\lambda_3(G) = \\frac{|V(G)|}{3} - 1$ and prove the inequality holds for $G$ strongly regular, a regular line graph or a Cayley graph on an abelian group. We also consider the extended problem of minimising $\\lambda_{n-1}$ on weighted graphs and reduce the existence of a minimiser with simple final eigenvalue to a vertex multiplication of a graph on 11 vertices. We prove that the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$ over weighted graphs is at most $O(\\sqrt{n})$ from the minimal $\\lambda_{n-1}$ over unweighted graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraph-Based Product Form (https://isaacg1.github.io/assets/graph-structure.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Graph-Based Product Form\nConjecture: Equivalence of broad and narrow second-level cuts\n26\nGraph-Based Product Form\nConjecture: Equivalence of broad and narrow second-level cuts\nWe conjecture that all broad second-level cuts have a corresponding narrow second-level\ncut, in the sense specified in Conjecture 10 below. As a result, we conjecture that the\nalgorithmic procedure described above discovers all components K1 and K2 that contain\nbroad second-level cuts: If there exist I ⊆K1 and J ⊆K2 which form a second-level cut,\nthen K1 and K2 are joint-ancestor free, and the above procedure will discover a narrow\nsecond-level cut between K1 and K2, even if that cut’s source is not necessarily (I, J) or\n(K1, K2).\n▶Conjecture 10. Consider a formal Markov chain G = (V, E). For each pair of connected\ncomponents K1 and K2 of C1(G), if there exist two nonempty sets I ⊆K1 and J ⊆K2\nsuch that I and J are joint-ancestor free in G, then we conjecture that K1 and K2 are\njoint-ancestor free in G. In other words, if there is a broad second-level cut with source (I, J),\nthen we conjecture that there is a narrow second-level cut arising from K1 and K2.\nIf Conjecture 10 holds, then we would be able to remove the distinction between broad and\nnarrow second level cuts.\nThe reason this claim is nontrivial is that in principle, K1 and K2 may have a joint\nancestor even if I and J are joint-ancestor free. However, we were not able to build such\nan example. Thus, to prove Conjecture 10, one must leverage the fact that K1 and K2 are\nconnected components of the cut graph C1(G).\nWe explored the following avenue towards proving this conjecture. Given joint-ancestor\nfree subsets I ⊆K1 and J ⊆K2, we further conjecture that there always exists an additional\nnode by which either I or J can be expanded, while preserving the joint-ancestor free\nproperty:\n▶Conjecture 11. Given two joint-ancestor free subsets I ⊆K1 and J ⊆K2, where either\nI ̸= K1, J ̸= K2, or both, there exists either\ni ∈K1\\I such that I ∪{i} and J are joint-ancestor free, or\nj ∈K2\\J such that I and J ∪{j} are joint-ancestor free.\nIf Conjecture 11 holds, then by applying it inductively we show K1 and K2 must be joint-\nancestor free in G whenever I and J are, which would complete the proof of Conjecture 10.\nIn Appendix B, we prove in Theorem 15 that in the special case where J contains a\nsingle vertex (|J| = 1), there exists an i ∈K1\\I such that I ∪{i} and J are joint-ancestor\nfree. However, we were not able to resolve the general conjecture, either Conjecture 11 or\nConjecture 10, and leave both as open problems.\n5\nExamples\nWe now give several examples of formal Markov chains arising from queueing systems which\nexhibit graph-based product form.\n5.1\nMultiserver jobs\nFirst, we give a practical example of a Markov chain with PS-product-form arising from its\ngraph structure. This setting is taken from [15] and explored in more detail in a technical\nreport [22].\nConsider the following multiserver-job (MSJ) queueing system. There are two types\nof jobs: class-1 jobs, which each require 3 servers to enter service, and class-2 jobs, which--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChords of longest cycles passing through a specified small set - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X26000995)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; In 1976, Thomassen raised the following famous conjecture, see [2], [4], [5]. ... Every longest cycle of a $3$-connected graph has a chord. ... Then among all the cycles containing $e$, each longest such cycle has a chord.\n## Keywords\n\nLongest cycles\n\n;\n\nChords\n\n;\n\n3-connected\n\n;\n\nPlanar graph\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn 1976, Thomassen raised the following famous conjecture, see [2], [4], [5].\n\nConjecture 1.1 Thomassen [1], [17]\n\nEvery longest cycle of a $3$-connected graph has a chord.\n\nAlthough the general conjecture remains unsolved, many partial results have been proved (see [1], [3], [10], [12], [14], [15], [17], [18], [19], [20], [21]). The first result regarding planar graphs was due to Zhang [21] in 1987, who showed that the conjecture holds for $3$-connected planar graphs which are either cubic or with minimum degree at least 4.\n\nTheorem 1.2 Zhang [21]\n\nLet $G$ be a $3$-connected planar graph which is either cubic or with minimum degree at least 4. Then any longest cycle of $G$ must have a chord.\n\nThomassen [18] showed that Conjecture 1.1 holds when $G$ is cubic. In 2018, Thomassen [19] further extended his result to $2$-connected cubic graphs. He also showed that if $G$ is $3$-connected and has minimum degree at least $4$, then some longest cycle in $G$ has a chord.\n\nTheorem 1.3 Thomassen [19]\n\nEvery longest cycle in a $2$-connected cubic graph has a chord.\n\nTheorem 1.4\n\n(Thomassen [19]) Let $G$ be a $3$-connected graph with minimum degree at least 4. Then some longest cycle of $G$ has a chord.\n\nRecently, Li and Liu generalized Theorem 1.3 as follows.\n\nTheorem 1.5 Li and Liu [13]\n\nIf $G$ is a $2$-connected cubic graph and $x,y$ are two distinct vertices of $G$, then every longest $(x,y)$-path of $G$ contains at least one internal vertex whose three neighbors are all in the path.\n\nConjecture 1.1 concerns the global structure in 3-connected graphs. In this paper, we consider the local structure in 3-connected graphs by studying longest cycles containing a specified small set. In 2005, Gu, Jia and Wu [9] proposed the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 1.6 Gu, Jia and Wu [9]\n\nLet $G$ be a $3$-connected graph and let $e$ be an edge of $G$. Then among all the cycles containing $e$, each longest such cycle has a chord.\n\nIn this paper, we study the existence of chords of longest cycles containing a specified small set and generalize Theorem 1.2, Theorem 1.3, Theorem 1.4, Theorem 1.5 as follows. Our results partially verify Conjecture 1.6.\n\nTheorem 1.7\n\nLet $G$ be a $2$-connected cubic graph and let $S$ be a specified set consisting of an edge plus a vertex. Then every longest cycle of $G$ containing $S$ must have a chord.\n\nBy Theorem 1.7, we obtain the following corollary.\n\nCorollary 1.8\n\nLet $G$ be a $2$-connected cubic graph and let $S$ be a specified set consisting of at most two vertices. Then every longest cycle of $G$ containing $S$ must have a chord.\n\nOur next result generalizes Theorem 1.4.\n\nTheorem 1.9\n\nLet $G$ be a $3$-connected graph with minimum degree at least 4 and let $e$ be a specified edge of $G$. Then some longest cycle of $G$ containing $e$ must have a chord.\n\nThe following result generalizes the corresponding theorem of Zhang [21].\n\nTheorem 1.10\n\nLet $G$ be a $3$-connected planar graph with minimum degree at least 4. Suppose $S$ is a specified set consisting of either three vertices or an edge plus a vertex. Then every longest cycle of $G$ containing $S$ must have a chord.\n\nBy Theorem 1.7, Theorem 1.10, we obtain the following generalization of Theorem 1.2.\n\nCorollary 1.11\n\nLet $G$ be a $3$-connected planar graph which is either cubic or with minimum degree at least 4 and let $S$ be a set consisting of an edge plus a vertex or a set with at most two vertices. Then every longest cycle of $G$ containing $S$ must have a chord.\n\nProofs of our main results will be given in Sections 3, 4 and 5. We use the techniques developed by Thomassen [19] to prove our first two main results. We use Bondy and Murty [6] for terminology and notation not defined here. For a graph $G=(V,E)$, we use $V\\left(G\\right)$ and $E\\left(G\\right)$ to denote the vertex set and the edge set, respectively. A chord of a cycle $C$ is an edge between two non-consecutive vertices of the cycle. We use $ch\\left(C\\right)$ to denote the number of chords of a cycle $C$. For a vertex $v\\in V\\left(G\\right)$, ${N}_{G}\\left(v\\right)$ is the set of all neighbors of $v$ and ${d}_{G}\\left(v\\right)=\\left|{N}_{G}\\left(v\\right)\\right|$. If $S\\subseteq V\\left(G\\right)$, then $G\\left[S\\right]$ denotes the subgraph induced by $S$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe question of avoidability for arrays was first raised by Häggkvist 